} note what the code refuses to do: retry non-retryable errors, retry forever, or retry without a cap. a retry loop that ignores those three rules is a self-inflicted denial of service. 4. circuit breakers: fail fast, recover carefully  thresholds matter more than the pattern. a breaker that opens after 3 failures out of 10 will flap on normal noise. use a ratio over a minimum sample: open when error rate exceeds 50% with at least 20 requests in 30 seconds, cool down for 10 seconds, then allow 3 trial requests. the point is to stop your threads, connections and memory being consumed by calls that cannot succeed. it does nothing for the failing service — that needs load shedding and backpressure on its side. 5. bulkheads: bound the blast radius a service pool of 100 database connections shared by three features is one pool with three ways to die. give each feature its own slice: 60 for the interactive path, 25 for background jobs, 15 for admin tools. when the nightly job saturates its 25, users still have 60. the same idea applies to thread pools per dependency, separate queues per tenant, and separate clusters per customer tier. you are choosing who gets hurt first, deliberately, instead of letting arrival order decide. 6. degrade on purpose  write this table down before the incident. deciding under pressure which data may be stale is how teams ship a financial bug.  7. review checklist

