function onSnapshot(snap) {
  // 1. Rewind to the server's authoritative state
  state = snap.state;

  // 2. Replay inputs the server has not acknowledged yet
  for (const input of pendingAfter(snap.lastAckedInput)) {
    state = simulate(state, input, TICK_DT);
  }

  // 3. Present smoothly: the renderer lerps toward the corrected state
  //    instead of teleporting the camera.
  renderer.blendToward(state, BLEND_MS);
}

hay:
netcode: ticks, prediction and the latency budget how a 100 ms round trip becomes a playable game, with the numbers behind it. trace input, simulation, broadcast and correction through a tick loop. choose a tick rate and compute the resulting input delay. explain what the client may predict safely and what it may not. estimate bandwidth per player and decide what needs interest management. light takes about 67 ms to cross the atlantic in fibre. no architecture removes that. netcode exists to hide the round trip from the player without handing authority to the client, because the client is the adversary. 1. the loop  every input is numbered. the server acknowledges the last input it applied. the client keeps unacknowledged inputs so it can replay them after a correction. that sequence number is the spine of the whole design. 2. tick rate, and what it really costs  doubling the tick rate doubles simulation cost and roughly doubles snapshot production. it does not halve the player's round trip. beyond about 60 hz the visible benefit is mostly hit registration, which is why competitive shooters pay for it and co-op games do not. 3. the latency budget, itemised take a 100 ms rtt player on a 30 hz server. one-way trip is 50 ms. the input waits for the next tick (up to 33 ms), the server simulates (say 2 ms), and the snapshot travels back (50 ms). that is up to 135 ms before the player sees their own action — enough to feel wrong. client-side prediction removes the two network legs for the local player: the client applies the input immediately and shows the result. what remains is correction when the server disagrees.  every predicted action must be correctable. if a prediction can produce an effect that cannot be undone — a purchase, a kill, a rank change — do not predict it. show a pending state instead. 4. reconciliation without visible snapping the server sends the authoritative state plus the last input it processed. the client compares that state with its own record of that tick, drops acknowledged inputs, replays the remainder, and blends the visual difference over a few frames. function onsnapshot(snap) {
  // 1. rewind to the server's authoritative state
  state = snap.state;

  // 2. replay inputs the server has not acknowledged yet
  for (const input of pendingafter(snap.lastackedinput)) {
    state = simulate(state, input, tick_dt);
  }

  // 3. present smoothly: the renderer lerps toward the corrected state
  //    instead of teleporting the camera.
  renderer.blendtoward(state, blend_ms);
} the blend is not cosmetic. a hard snap of 40 cm is read by players as a bug or as cheating; the same correction spread over 100 ms is read as normal movement. 5. other players are interpolated, not predicted you cannot know another player's next input, so their movement is rendered slightly in the past — typically 100 ms behind the newest snapshot — and interpolated between the two most recent states. this trades a little accuracy for smooth motion despite jitter and packet loss. extrapolation is the alternative: predict where they are going. it looks better on a clean network and much worse when someone stops, turns, or lags. most games interpolate; a few extrapolate with a short cap. 6. bandwidth: do the multiplication before the design review sixty-four players, 20 snapshots per second, 60 bytes of state per player: 64 × 20 × 60 = 76,800 bytes per second per client, about 77 kb/s down — 0.6 mbit/s. that is fine. now make it 200 players in an open world: 240 kb/s per client, and the server is producing 200 × 200 × 20 × 60 bytes ≈ 48 mb/s of egress.  head-of-line blocking means one lost packet stalls every later packet, including fresher state that supersedes it. games send state over udp with their own sequencing, and use reliable channels only for events that must not be lost.  7. review checklist

