// Pure function: tickets + profile -> proposed matches with a score.
function matchFunction(tickets, profile) {
  const proposals = [];
  const sorted = tickets.sort(byEnqueueTime);   // fairness first

  for (const anchor of sorted) {
    if (anchor.used) continue;
    const candidates = tickets
      .filter(t => !t.used && t.id !== anchor.id)
      .filter(t => t.region === anchor.region)
      .filter(t => Math.abs(t.rating - anchor.rating) <= anchor.band)
      .sort((a, b) => Math.abs(a.rating - anchor.rating) - Math.abs(b.rating - anchor.rating));

    const team = [anchor, ...candidates.slice(0, profile.playersPerMatch - 1)];
    if (team.length < profile.playersPerMatch) continue;

    team.forEach(t => { t.used = true; });
    proposals.push({
      tickets: team.map(t => t.id),
      quality: qualityScore(team),        // 0..1, higher is better
      latency: worstPing(team),
    });
  }
  return proposals.filter(p => p.quality >= profile.minQuality);
}

hay:
matchmaking by design tickets, pools, quality functions and the queue-time trade you cannot avoid. trace a ticket from submission to assigned game server. design pool expansion that trades match quality for queue time deliberately. write a match function with an explicit quality metric. size the matcher from queue depth and arrival rate. perfect matches exist in theory and never in your queue. every design decision is about how fast to widen the search before the player leaves. 1. the pipeline  the ticket carries what the matcher needs: player id, skill rating, region, party size, mode, and enqueue time. the pool is the set of tickets eligible to be matched together. the profile declares the match shape — 5v5, one region, rating band. the match function is the only custom code, and it returns proposed matches with a quality score. finding ten compatible players and finding a machine to host them are different problems with different failure modes. keep the match function pure: given tickets and a profile, propose matches. let the director handle allocation, retries and player connection details. 2. the unavoidable trade queue time and match quality pull in opposite directions. tighten the rating band and the queue gets longer; widen it and stomp rates rise. you cannot escape this, so make it explicit and measurable.  a 200 ms match is not a worse version of a good match; it is unplayable for a competitive game. widen skill first, then backfill with bots, then let the player leave. latency has a hard ceiling; rating does not. 3. sizing the matcher with arithmetic suppose 2,000 players are queued in a mode, matches need 10 players, and arrivals are 40 players per second. a pool with 2,000 candidates produces 200 potential matches instantly if the band is wide enough; at ±50 rating only about 3% of candidates qualify, so the effective pool is 60 players — six matches, roughly 15 seconds of arrivals.  for this mode, an empty pool means 10 / 40 = 0.25 s between qualifying arrivals, so waits are dominated by search quality rather than supply — which is why band widening matters more than matcher speed. in a low-population mode at 2 players per second, the same match takes 5 s of arrivals: supply is the problem, and no algorithm fixes it. that is when you backfill. 4. the match function // pure function: tickets + profile -> proposed matches with a score.
